    <title>Daily Digest 2026-09-05: numbat - AI agent observability, (Fri, Sep 4th); AI Coding Agents Are Installing Unknown/Untrusted ; Show HN: Rubato – Retro-Mac desk device mirrors AI</title>
    <updated>2026-09-05T00:00:00Z</updated>
    <link href="https://islandirie.github.io/my-rss-feed/daily/2026-09-05.html"/>
    <content type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;numbat - AI agent observability, (Fri, Sep 4th)&lt;/b&gt; — Agentic tools are changing how software gets built — worth knowing what's newly available and battle-tested. &lt;a href="https://isc.sans.edu/diary/rss/33312"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;AI Coding Agents Are Installing Unknown/Untrusted Code on Corporate Networks&lt;/b&gt; — Agentic tools are changing how software gets built — worth knowing what's newly available and battle-tested. &lt;a href="https://www.schneier.com/blog/archives/2026/09/ai-coding-agents-are-installing-unknown-untrusted-code-on-corporate-networks.html"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Show HN: Rubato – Retro-Mac desk device mirrors AI coding state-ESP8266&lt;/b&gt; — New or updated LLM releases affect tool choices across your entire stack — from IDE copilots to pipeline automation. &lt;a href="https://github.com/lovaxi/Rubato_Device"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;OpenAI's rogue agents were caught communicating via public wikis&lt;/b&gt; — Benchmarks cut through the hype — useful for choosing which model or tool to actually rely on. &lt;a href="https://simonwillison.net/2026/Sep/4/rogue-agent-wikis/"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Show HN: Moadim.io – A scheduler for agents&lt;/b&gt; — Expanding what LLMs can interact with — relevant if you're building or using agentic pipelines. &lt;a href="https://moadim.io/"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Kubernetes isn’t new, but AI makes It scary again&lt;/b&gt; — Kubernetes ecosystem changes can surface new operational patterns or affect your existing clusters. &lt;a href="https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/09/04/kubernetes-isnt-new-but-ai-makes-it-scary-again/"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;How to build a secure-by-default AI coding agent&lt;/b&gt; — Supply chain risks are often overlooked until it's too late — track these proactively. &lt;a href="https://stackoverflow.blog/2026/09/04/how-to-build-a-secure-by-default-ai-coding-agent/"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Show HN: Free macOS App to import photos from memory card with file naming rules&lt;/b&gt; — Community-vetted signal from Hacker News — high upvote-to-age ratio means the engineering community found it worth reading. &lt;a href="https://github.com/jianfenglin-git/photo-batch-importer"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;The Pelican comparison grid for Astra is pretty interesting&lt;/b&gt; — New or updated LLM releases affect tool choices across your entire stack — from IDE copilots to pipeline automation. &lt;a href="https://simonwillison.net/2026/Sep/4/astra-pelicans/"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CPU + GPU: Why AI platform engineering is a heterogeneous infrastructure problem&lt;/b&gt; — Flagged as relevant to your interests — read the summary to decide if it's worth your time. &lt;a href="https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/09/04/cpu-gpu-why-ai-platform-engineering-is-a-heterogeneous-infrastructure-problem/"&gt;Read&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</content>
